--TEST--
Spec § 4: `Core\Time\Duration::parse` is `rule:types/duration`'s second entry point into the literal's own grammar, so every refusal is a `ParseError` naming the rule it broke
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A duration literal's refusals are compile errors, so the only place a
// program can watch the grammar say no is `parse` -- `rule:types/duration`'s second
// entry point into the one parser, printing the same sentence the lexer does.
// Every row below is a rule from § 1, in the order that section states them.
array<string> $refused = [
    "",                      // the grammar has no empty spelling
    "ms",                    // a unit with nothing in front of it
    "1h30",                  // a count that runs out before its unit
    "30x",                   // a spelling the eight units do not have
    "30S",                   // the right unit in the wrong case (`rule:classes/reserved-spellings-are-lower-case`)
    "1H30M",                 // named by the first mis-cased unit, not the last
    "30m1h",                 // units strictly descend
    "1h1h",                  // and may not repeat, which is the same rule
    "-7d",                   // no sign anywhere: a backwards step is `negated`
    "1.5s",                  // no fraction: the finer unit is the spelling
    "30 seconds",            // and no separator, so the space stops it
];
foreach ($refused as string $text) {
    try {
        echo Core\Time\Duration::parse($text)->toString(), "|\n";
    } catch (ParseError $bad) {
        echo $bad->message, "|\n";
    }
}

// The class is `ParseError` and not merely a `Throwable`: what failed is a
// text that will not read. `ParseError` descends from `RuntimeError`, so the
// narrower clause is written first and it is the one that answers.
//
// Behind a binding, because `rule:expressions/intrinsic-list-is-closed` folds this member's literal argument
// and a malformed one written inline is a compile error rather than a throw.
string $unreadable = "nope";
try {
    echo Core\Time\Duration::parse($unreadable)->toString(), "|\n";
} catch (ParseError $narrow) {
    echo "parse|\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $wide) {
    echo "runtime|\n";
}

// Everything the grammar *does* accept survives the round trip, because
// `toString` is this same table read backwards: a canonical text comes back
// unchanged, and zero has the one spelling the grammar cannot write as empty.
echo Core\Time\Duration::parse("1w2d3h4m5s6ms7us8ns")->toString(), "|\n";
echo Core\Time\Duration::parse("0s")->toString(), "|\n";
echo Core\Time\Duration::parse("0s")->compareTo(0s) as string, "|\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Time\Duration::parse(): a duration is empty|
Core\Time\Duration::parse(): a duration unit needs a count before it|
Core\Time\Duration::parse(): a duration count needs a unit after it|
Core\Time\Duration::parse(): `x` is not a duration unit — the units are `ns`, `us`, `ms`, `s`, `m`, `h`, `d` and `w`|
Core\Time\Duration::parse(): a duration unit is lower case only, so `S` is `s` (`rule:classes/names-resolve-case-sensitively`)|
Core\Time\Duration::parse(): a duration unit is lower case only, so `H` is `h` (`rule:classes/names-resolve-case-sensitively`)|
Core\Time\Duration::parse(): duration units strictly descend and may not repeat, so `h` may not follow `m`|
Core\Time\Duration::parse(): duration units strictly descend and may not repeat, so `h` may not follow `h`|
Core\Time\Duration::parse(): `-` has no meaning in a duration|
Core\Time\Duration::parse(): `.` has no meaning in a duration|
Core\Time\Duration::parse(): ` ` has no meaning in a duration|
parse|
1w2d3h4m5s6ms7us8ns|
0s|
0|
